{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 40-year-old man presents for evaluation with his wife, who reports that her husband has become increasingly restless over the past 2 years. Recently, he has been making jerky, dancelike movements with his arms and legs and is unable to control his extremities during these episodes. His wife also reports that the patient has become forgetful and has trouble completing tasks. Which of the following region of gene has mutation in this patient?", "c": ["Intron", "5’ UTR (Untranslated region)", "3’ UTR", "Exon", "Telomere"], "a": 3, "e": "Chorea and cognitive decline are HD, a CAG expansion in an exon.", "w": ["Friedreich.", "Fragile X.", "Myotonic dystrophy.", "", "Not repeat disorders."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 37-year-old man comes to the office for a follow-up appointment. He was diagnosed with depression at the last visit two months ago. He now complains that he has had a two week history of uncontrolled arm movements. He is concerned about this as his father had these movements before he died at the age of 45. He has been told that his grandmother also had these symptoms before she died at the age of 53. The description above demonstrates which of the following?", "c": ["Anticipation", "Codominance", "Imprinting", "Incomplete penetrance", "Variable expression"], "a": 0, "e": "Onset at earlier ages in successive generations is anticipation.", "w": ["", "Not shown.", "Not shown.", "Not shown.", "Not the pattern described."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 32-year-old woman is brought to your office by her husband. The husband says that she had been acting strange lately. She has been forgetful, and she sometimes becomes angered for no reason, which is highly unusual for her. She has also been having random, uncontrollable movements, which are also new. On examination, she appears withdrawn and flat. On further questioning, she reveals that her father died at age 45 from a movement disorder. Which of the following is the pathological hallmark of the patient's condition?", "c": ["Substantia nigra pars compacta", "Alpha-synuclein intracellular inclusions", "Loss of neurons in the caudate nucleus and putamen", "Lipohyalinosis", "Beta-amyloid plaques"], "a": 2, "e": "Huntington disease shows neuronal loss in the caudate and putamen.", "w": ["Parkinson.", "Parkinson.", "", "Hypertensive small vessels.", "Alzheimer."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A patient with chorea and dementia is diagnosed with Huntington’s Disease. Which of the following correctly describes the genetic abnormality present in this patient?", "c": ["Disruption in HOX gene expression", "Expansion of CAG repeats on Chromosome 4", "Missense mutation in p-App gene", "Mutation in the APOE4 allele", "X-linked recessive trait"], "a": 1, "e": "HD is a CAG expansion in HTT on chromosome 4.", "w": ["Not HD.", "", "Alzheimer.", "Alzheimer risk.", "HD is autosomal dominant."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 7 year old boy is brought to the clinic for evaluation due to his difficulties reading. His teacher has reported in parent-teacher conferences that the child is unable to keep up with the rest of the class. His mother explains that generally he is very well behaved, but he does have the odd tendency of flapping his hands when he gets nervous. Physical exam shows a high forehead, prominent supraorbital ridges, protruding ears, and a prominent jaw. Formal testing shows that the patient has an IQ of 69, which appears consistent with his academic performance. What is the most likely diagnosis that can explain these findings?", "c": ["Down’s Syndrome", "Fragile X syndrome", "Frederic’s ataxia", "Angelman syndrome", "Prader willi syndrome"], "a": 1, "e": "Intellectual disability, hand flapping, long face, prominent jaw and protruding ears are fragile X.", "w": ["Different features.", "", "Ataxia.", "Seizures and happy demeanor.", "Hyperphagia and hypotonia."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 12-year-old boy is brought in by his parents for evaluation of developmental delay. On general inspection, the patient appears to have a long face, a large jaw, and large everted eyes. When talking to the patient, the physician notices that the patient avoids eye contact and keeps on repeating the same words. The mother mentions that there are other family members with similar symptoms. Genetic analysis reveals the patient has an X-linked defect. Which cardiac abnormality is most associated with this patient's condition?", "c": ["Atrial septal defect", "Mitral valve prolapse", "Patent ductus arteriosus", "Transposition of the great vessels", "Truncus arteriosus"], "a": 1, "e": "Fragile X is associated with mitral valve prolapse.", "w": ["Not typical.", "", "Not typical.", "Not typical.", "Not typical."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 3-year-old boy is brought to a pediatrician. His past medical history is unknown, as this is his first visit to the pediatrician. His mother reports that the family prefers an isolated existence and therefore has not sought medical help. However, she reports being concerned about his learning abilities, as his speech appears delayed compared to his sisters. On physical exam, the boy has a prominently long face, large jaw, and large ears. The pediatrician recommends a genetic test to assess one of the following?", "c": ["CGG repeat in 3’ UTR", "CAG repeat in 5’ UTR", "CGG repeat in 5’ UTR", "CAG repeat in 3’ UTR", "CAG repeat in exonic region"], "a": 2, "e": "Fragile X is a CGG expansion in the 5′ UTR of FMR1.", "w": ["Wrong location.", "Wrong repeat.", "", "Wrong repeat.", "Huntington."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 4-year-old girl is brought to the pediatrician for evaluation of learning issues. Her mother reports that there is a family history of intellectual disability in the men in her family, but she is unsure what that syndrome is. On physical exam, the patient has a mildly long face and slightly prominent ears. Tests show that her speech and language skills are delayed for her age. She is referred to speech and language therapist as well as genetic testing. Which of the following genetic changes has led to this disorder?", "c": ["Large deletion of end of X-chromosome", "Hypermethylation of CpG island at promotor region of gene", "Missense mutation", "Nonsense mutation", "Frame shift mutation"], "a": 1, "e": "Fragile X: CGG expansion hypermethylates the CpG island at the FMR1 promoter, silencing it.", "w": ["Not the mechanism.", "", "Not the mechanism.", "Not the mechanism.", "Not the mechanism."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 14-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department after losing consciousness. He had no preceding chest pain or palpitations. His father has cataracts and had frontal balding in his twenties but has no history of cardiac disease. His paternal grandfather also had early-onset balding. Physical examination shows frontal hair loss, temporal muscle wasting, and testicular atrophy. Neurologic examination shows bilateral foot drop and weakness of the intrinsic hand muscles. An ECG shows bradycardia with third-degree atrioventricular block. The severity of this patient's symptoms compared to that of his father is most likely due to which of the following genetic properties?", "c": ["Pleiotropy", "Loss of heterozygosity", "Codominance", "Penetrance", "Anticipation"], "a": 4, "e": "Myotonic dystrophy shows anticipation, so the son is more severely affected than the father.", "w": ["Not the explanation.", "Tumor genetics.", "Not relevant.", "Not the explanation.", ""], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 35-year-old man presents to his primary care physician's office for difficulty chewing and walking and mild muscular pain. He notices himself dragging his feet while walking and pain in his legs. He experiences stiffness in his jaw and his hands. On physical exam, there is bilateral ptosis and atrophy of the temporalis muscle. There is weakness with turning of the neck and bilateral dorsiflexion. A serum creatine kinase is normal. Genetic testing shows one of the following", "c": ["CTG nucleotide expansion on the DMPK gene on chromosome 19", "CTG nucleotide expansion on the DMPK gene on chromosome 9", "CGG nucleotide expansion on the DMPK gene on chromosome 19", "CTG nucleotide expansion on the FMR1 gene on chromosome 19", "GAA nucleotide expansion on the fratraxin gene on chromosome 9"], "a": 0, "e": "Myotonic dystrophy is a CTG expansion in DMPK on chromosome 19.", "w": ["", "Wrong chromosome.", "Wrong repeat.", "Wrong gene.", "Friedreich."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 5 year old girl is brought to the clinic because of intermittent muscle cramping that has been occurring for the past 4 months. The episodes last from a couple of seconds to a few minutes. They are relived by gentle massage and other comfort measures. She has had mild developmental motor delay since birth, however physical, occupational, and speech therapy has helped improve her skills. Her father has a similar condition, where he experiences muscle cramping after exercising or heavy lifting. The patient is awake and alert upon physical exam. A neurological exam shows mild muscle weakness distally, normal deep tendon reflexes, and no Babinksi sign bilaterally. She has difficulty relaxing her grip after squeezing the clinician’s fingers. She is able to answer questions correctly, but has marked impaired enunciation. What is the likely pathogenesis in this patient?", "c": ["Expression of abnormal protein", "Repression of protein", "Toxic mRNA", "Decreased Transcription", "Unstable mRNA"], "a": 2, "e": "Myotonia with an affected father points to myotonic dystrophy, caused by toxic CUG-repeat mRNA.", "w": ["Polyglutamine disorders.", "Fragile X and Friedreich.", "", "Not the mechanism.", "Not the mechanism."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 15-year-old Caucasian male presents to your office with gait instability and frequent falls. Physical examination reveals kyphoscoliosis, pes cavus, and lower extremity ataxia. Position and joint sensation are also impaired. If the symptoms described are from an inherited disease, this patient is most likely to die of which of the following?", "c": ["Liver failure", "Cardiomyopathy", "Renal disease", "Intracranial bleeding", "Aortic dissection", "Brain tumor"], "a": 1, "e": "Friedreich ataxia patients most often die of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.", "w": ["Not typical.", "", "Not typical.", "Not typical.", "Marfan.", "Not typical."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 16 year old boy is brought to the clinic for the evaluation of an unstable gait and speech difficulty that is getting worse over time. His past medical history is unremarkable, he is not taking any medications, he has no allergies, and he denies smoking/alcohol usage. His blood pressure is 120/80 mm Hg, pulse is 75/min, and temperature is 98.7°F. A musculoskeletal exam reveals the presence of scoliosis and hammer toes. His neurological exam is notable for dysarthria, dysmetria, nystagmus, and the absence of plantar reflexes bilaterally on his lower extremities. What is the likely cause of presentation in this patient?", "c": ["CGG repeat expansion at 5’UTR", "GAA repeat expansion at exonic region", "GAA repeat expansion at intronic region", "GAA repeat expansion at 3’UTR", "CTG repeat expansion at 5’UTR"], "a": 2, "e": "Ataxia, absent reflexes and scoliosis are Friedreich ataxia, a GAA expansion in an intron of FXN.", "w": ["Fragile X.", "Wrong location.", "", "Wrong location.", "Wrong repeat."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false},
{"t": "Trinucleotide Repeat Disorders", "s": "A 13-year-old male from Switzerland presents with clumsiness and constant falls. These symptoms have progressively worsened for the past 8 months. On physical examination, there is both upper and lower extremity limb ataxia and lower extremity weakness, with an absence of patellar and ankle deep tendon reflexes. The patient has a deficit on position and vibration sense, with intact pain and temperature sensation. Kyphoscoliosis is appreciated on exam. An MRI of the brain and spine is performed, which does not show cerebellar atrophy but atrophy in the spinal cord and medulla. Which of the following is the cause of this disorder?", "c": ["Expression of abnormal protein", "Repression of protein expression", "Toxic mRNA", "Decreased replication", "Unstable mRNA"], "a": 1, "e": "Friedreich ataxia: the intronic GAA expansion represses frataxin expression.", "w": ["Polyglutamine disorders.", "", "Myotonic dystrophy.", "Not the mechanism.", "Not the mechanism."], "src": "sq", "lq": 10, "note": "From the school's practice quiz. The quiz review has no answer key, so the answer and explanation were supplied for this site.", "viz": false}
